    ":question/id": "38df000e-738f-5d02-8231-0fba0871726b",
    ":question/problem": "Express $\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
