    ":question/id": "7d2e5f2f-dd16-5fbd-88cd-c72fe86e0b28",
    ":question/problem": "Express $\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =0\\,\\operatorname{sin}x+1\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
